  <section id="concept" class="hyper-section"><span class="hyper-index">01 / RESEARCH QUESTION</span><div><h2>Hyperarchiviation / <em>studies.</em></h2><p>RESEARCH OBJECT: hyperarchiviation as a situated artistic practice. Investigating how autonomous physical-digital archives can sustain spaces of resistance, self-education, shared memory and collective inquiry beyond dominant institutional and platform infrastructures.</p><p>CASE STUDIES: Archive of the Untamed / struggles for urban commons, spontaneous ecologies and collective memory; ALMA Futura / feminist self-education, research into bodies and technologies, and autonomous knowledge infrastructures. Two situated practices of resistance informing an evolving methodology.</p><p>QUESTION: How can autonomous archives become spaces of resistance in which physical objects, digital infrastructures and collective relationships continuously reshape one another? What forms of care, deceleration and shared governance can sustain them?</p></div><div class="hyper-protocol"><div><span>01 / OBJECT</span><p>Develop methods for collecting, hosting, storing, sharing and activating situated knowledge, as autonomous alternatives to dominant digital platforms.</p></div><div><span>02 / COUNTER-DATA</span><p>Digital records, material research objects, situated accounts and collective practices. Each context requires its own archival protocols.</p></div><div><span>03 / QUESTION</span><p>Who hosts, maintains and contributes? What happens when the archive can become an educational and physical device?</p></div></div></section>
  <section id="untamed" class="hyper-section hyper-previous"><span class="hyper-index">02 / CASE STUDIES & COLLABORATORS</span><div><h2>Situated struggles / <em>shared research.</em></h2><p>ARCHIVE OF THE UNTAMED + EXIT / PARTICIPATORY RESEARCH — ALMA FUTURA / ARTIST COLLABORATORS</p><div class="hyper-genealogy"><div class="hyper-genealogy-item"><span>01 / ARCHIVE OF THE UNTAMED</span><div><h3>Urban commons / collective memory / resistance</h3><p>A participatory archive developed through artistic and collective practices around spontaneous urban ecologies, contested public space and struggles for access. Mapping, gatherings and temporary interventions connect digital memory with situated action.</p></div></div><div class="hyper-genealogy-item"><span>02 / ALMA FUTURA — ARTIST COLLABORATORS</span><div><h3>Feminist self-education / bodies / technologies</h3><p>ALMA Futura develops collective research and self-education on women, bodies and technologies, including research into vaginal fluids where institutional access to study is obstructed. Their proposed hyperarchive explores an independent space for research materials, shared learning and resistance through autonomous technological infrastructure.</p></div></div><div class="hyper-genealogy-item"><span>03 / SHARED RESEARCH QUESTIONS</span><div><h3>Autonomy / deceleration / collective care</h3><p>Who controls the infrastructure of knowledge? How can collectives set their own research rhythms, maintain their materials and make them accessible on their own terms? How do archives move between digital interfaces, physical devices and collective action?</p></div></div></div><p class="hyper-pink-note">★ WHEN PRESERVATION IS NO LONGER POSSIBLE, HOW CAN WE RESIST THROUGH CARE AND KEEP KNOWLEDGE IN MOTION?</p><div class="hyper-previous-links">
